//! ## Start here
//!
//! [`fn_80600160`] is the smallest complete entry: it links one registry node.
//! [`fn_80600180`] walks the same list. For gameplay, start with
//! [`fn_80601380`] (arrow state machine) or [`fn_80601d80`] (select-screen
//! constructor), then follow the calls down to [`fn_80602460`]
//! (choose the first free player slot) and [`fn_80603260`] (preview cycling).
//! For the state-object family, start with [`fn_806510c0`] (the one explicit
//! `daBigShell_c::StateID_Wait` registration) and then read
//! [`fn_806511d0`] (its destructor) and [`fn_80651390`] (the adjacent
//! `0x3E0`-byte constructor). For wave 5, start with [`fn_80634cf0`] and
//! [`fn_8063f850`] (the two select-screen tables), then [`fn_807a4930`]
//! (Yoshi-egg states) and [`fn_80735030`] (ship gear). For the wave-4 stage
//! actors, [`fn_8066a820`] (cannon pipe states) and [`fn_80759c60`] (2-D
//! player states) are the two largest state tables; [`fn_80725bc0`] and
//! [`fn_80727d60`] are the POW and
//! prop block tables. For wave 7, start with [`fn_80679200`]
//! (`daElasticKinoko_c`, nine states) or [`fn_8073e560`] (`daStrongBox_c`,
//! eight states); [`fn_807531d0`] and [`fn_807540e0`] are the two largest
//! water-actor constructors and [`fn_80730af0`] shows the rotating-pipe shape.
//! For wave 9, [`fn_806e0fb0`] is the smallest complete table example and
//! [`fn_80787630`] the largest; [`fn_80721030`] and [`fn_80787990`] are the
//! two in-place constructors. For wave 10, start with [`fn_80664ba0`] (five
//! Koopa Jr demo states) or [`fn_80666260`] (the rubble timing block);
//! [`fn_80778c20`] is the largest table (nine sand-pillar states), and
//! [`fn_806f9e20`] shows the recovered in-place model initialiser. For wave 11,
//! [`fn_806a0b30`] is the largest new table (eight clown states) and
