/// Dispatches the state-indexed interface descriptor at `0x807CEE4C` and
/// publishes the actor transform.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80762890` at `0x80762890` (156 bytes)
/// - Synthetic base: `0x80600000`; this is not a runtime address.
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/rel-d_basesNP/export/80762890_FUN_80762890.c`
///
/// Raw words for the dispatcher call:
///
/// ```text
/// 807628d4  lwz  r0,0x254(r30)  ; state word from the actor
/// 807628d8  lis  r4,0x807d
/// 807628dc  addi r4,r4,-4532    ; 0x807CEE4C descriptor table base
/// 807628e0  mr   r3,r30         ; receiver = actor
/// 807628e4  mulli r0,r0,12      ; 12-byte descriptor stride
/// 807628e8  add  r12,r4,r0      ; r12 = 0x807CEE4C + 12 * state
/// 807628ec  bl   0x802dcbfc
/// ```
///
/// So the literal `0x807CEE4C` in the Ghidra pseudo-argument list is **not** an
/// `r4` payload: it is the base of the descriptor table from which `r12` is
/// formed, and `r4` at the `bl` is dead. The table is indexed by the actor's
/// `+0x254` word (the same word the neighbour `0x80762C40` clears to zero),
/// with a 12-byte stride.
///
/// Shipped dispatcher `0x802DCBFC` (re-read from `work/dol/main.elf`):
///
/// ```text
/// 802dcbfc  lwz   r0,0x0(r12)   ; self_adjust
/// 802dcc00  lwz   r11,0x4(r12)  ; index (signed)
/// 802dcc04  lwz   r12,0x8(r12)  ; table offset, or fn address when index < 0
/// 802dcc08  add   r3,r3,r0
/// 802dcc0c  cmpwi cr0,r11,0
/// 802dcc10  bt    0x802dcc1c    ; BT BI=0 -> branch if index < 0
/// 802dcc14  lwzx  r12,r3,r12
/// 802dcc18  lwzx  r12,r12,r11
/// 802dcc1c  mtspr 288,r12
/// 802dcc20  bcctr
/// ```
///
/// The guard is a signed `index < 0` test, not `index != 0`: a negative entry
/// uses `[r12+8]` as the function address itself. The single populated entry
/// (`state == 0`) is `{self_adjust = 0, index = -1, table = 0x80762C50}`
/// (`0x807CEE4C = 0`, `0x807CEE50 = 0xFFFFFFFF`,
/// `0x807CEE54 = 0x80762C50`), i.e. a direct call to the `blr` stub at
/// `0x80762C50`. That is why this body models the dispatcher with the signed
/// test instead of [`interface_call_desc`], whose `index == 0` shortcut does
/// not match the shipped branch.
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `actor` - the object; its vtable pointer is at `+0x60`, its state word at
///   `+0x254`, its position at `+0xAC..+0xB4` and its part at `+0x1AC`.
///
/// # Returns
/// Always `1`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Dispatches the class callback `+0x60` with the current player and its
/// character byte, calls the state interface handler once, then runs the
/// position update `0x80762CE0`, the part tick `0x80762B20` and the transform
/// writer [`fn_80762b30`].
///
/// # Safety
/// `actor` must be live; the player manager `0x8042A1AC` must be initialised and
/// the descriptor at `0x807CEE4C + 12 * [actor+0x254]` must be readable.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - `mulli r0,r0,12` / `add r12,r4,r0` with `r4 = 0x807CEE4C` and
/// `r3 = actor` are literal, the signed `bt` guard was re-read from
/// `main.elf`, and the `state == 0` entry at `0x807CEE4C` decodes to
/// `{0, -1, 0x80762C50}`, which is consistent with that guard.

pub unsafe fn fn_806fc9c0() {
    let object = abi_call1!(0x8016_28C0, 0x398) as usize;
    if object != 0 {
        // unknown: 0x806FCA20 installs vtable 0x80803330 and registers the
        // global 0x8082F078; it is still untranslated, so it stays address-only.
        abi_call1!(0x806F_CA20, object);
    }
}

